.TH TPA 1 "Juni 2026" "TPA Manual" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tpa \- Minimalist package builder and repository bundler for .deb archives.

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tpa
[COMMANDS] [OPTIONS]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tpa
is a high-performance, minimalist tool designed for automating the creation of Debian packages (.deb) and managing apt-compatible repositories. It is built for efficiency, allowing package definitions via JSON blueprints or direct command-line arguments.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
Initialize a new project structure.
.TP
.B build
Build a .deb package from input files and metadata.
.TP
.B parse
Parse existing package metadata.
.TP
.B pack
Create or update an apt repository index.
.TP
.B json
Generate a JSON blueprint from command-line arguments.
.TP
.B schema
Output the schema definition for JSON blueprints.

.SH OPTIONS
.SS General Options
.TP
.BI -in " directory"
Input directory containing source files (default: ".").
.TP
.BI -out " directory"
Output directory for the generated .deb file (default: ".").

.SS Package Metadata
.TP
.BI -name " string"
Package name (default: "myNewAPTPackage").
.TP
.BI -ver " string"
Package version (default: "0.0.1").
.TP
.BI -desc " string"
Short description.
.TP
.BI -maintainer " string"
Maintainer contact info.
.TP
.BI -section " string"
Package section (default: "utils").

.SS Dependencies & Relationships
.TP
.BI -depends " string"
Package dependencies (comma separated).
.TP
.BI -conflicts " string"
Conflicting packages.

.SS Repository Options
.TP
.BI -arch " string"
Architecture (default: "all").
.TP
.BI -gpg " ID"
GPG Key ID for signing (empty for no signing).

.SH EXAMPLE CONFIGURATION
The following is an example of a valid control file structure as utilized by 
.B tpa
itself, including advanced relationship fields:

.nf
Package: tpa
Version: 0.0.1
Architecture: amd64
Maintainer: Eugen Lupricht <eugen@lupricht.net>
Description: Automation tool for Debian packages and repository bundling
 tpa is a minimalist package builder and repository bundler for .deb archives.
 .
 As a standalone tool, tpa decouples packaging from system structure and
 enables the efficient orchestration of software stacks.
Depends: libc6, dpkg, gpg, gzip
Recommends: git, make
Suggests: bash-completion
Breaks: old-tpa-version
Replaces: old-tpa-version
Homepage: https://github.com/eugen252009/tpa
Section: utils
Essential: no
.fi
.SH EXAMPLE USAGE
The following workflow demonstrates the complete lifecycle of creating a 
package and bundling it into an apt repository. Optional GPG signing can 
be enabled by removing the comment symbol (#):

.nf
# 1. Initialize the package structure
tpa init -name=helloDeb -ver=0.1 -desc="Short description" -maintainer="Maintainer" -section=utils -out=hello_deb

# 2. Build the .deb package
tpa build -in=hello_deb -out="hello_deb/hello_deb.deb"

# 3. Bundle into an apt repository (optional: uncomment for GPG signing)
tpa pack -in=hello_deb -out=repo # -gpg=YOUR_GPG_KEY_ID
.fi

.SH EXAMPLE OUTPUT
.nf
Initializing package 'helloDeb' in: hello_deb
Package structure for './helloDeb' created.
dpkg-deb: building package 'hellodeb' in 'hello_deb/hello_deb.deb'.
Package myNewAPTPackage.deb successfully created!
Repo build complete!
.fi

.SH TPA DEPENDENCIES:
.I tpa 
is a lean tool that relies on the following core system utilities to function:
.IP \(bu 2
.B libc6: 
Standard C library (for runtime execution).
.IP \(bu 2
.B dpkg: 
Required for the actual .deb packaging process.
.IP \(bu 2
.B gpg: 
Required only if you intend to sign your repositories.
.IP \(bu 2
.B gzip: 
Used for compression of package data.

